The functions of the biracial committee shall be to:
(1) Foster mutual understanding and respect among all racial, religious and ethnic groups in the city;
(2) Encourage equality of treatment for and prevent discrimination against any racial, religious or ethnic group or its members;
(3) Cooperate with governmental and nongovernmental agencies and organizations which have like or kindred functions; and
(4) Make such investigations and studies in the field of human relations as in the judgment of the committee will aid in effectuating its general purposes.
(Code 1991, § 22-106)

144:
The powers and duties of the biracial committee shall be to:
(1) Work together with federal, state and city agencies in developing courses of instruction for presentation to city employees and in public and private schools, public libraries, museums and other suitable places, on techniques for achieving harmonious intergroup relations within the city;
(2) Enlist the cooperation of the various racial, religious or ethnic groups or their members;
(3) Study and make recommendations concerning the problems of prejudice, intolerance, bigotry, discrimination and disorder in all or any fields of human relationship;
(4) Receive and investigate complaints and initiate its own investigations of racial, religious and ethnic group tensions, prejudice, intolerance, bigotry, and disorder or discrimination against any person, group of persons, organizations or corporation, whether practiced by private persons, associations or corporations;
(5) Hold hearings, request the attendance of witnesses, take the testimony of any person under oath and in connection therewith to require the production of any evidence relating to any matter under investigation or in request before the committee. No public hearing shall be held without written approval of the mayor and city council. The powers encumbered in this subsection may be exercised by any group of three or more members of the committee when so authorized in writing by the committee. The committee after the completion of any hearing shall make a report in writing to the mayor and city council, setting forth the facts found by it and its recommendations. If the committee finds that there has been a violation of an existing ordinance, it shall recommend to the mayor that he refer the matter to the legal department for appropriate legal action. If the committee finds that there is no existing ordinance that prohibits such discrimination, it shall recommend to the mayor that an ordinance be enacted by the mayor and city council prohibiting such discrimination. At any hearing before the committee or any of its committees, a witness shall have the right to be advised by counsel present at such hearings.
(Code 1991, § 22-107)

